#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133516 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1cae73a3-842e-5a1a-8774-35bba342845c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a Subspace Spanned by Orthogonal Vectors"}] :question/id #uuid "89a4773c-f6bb-577d-8743-5d0a46c1da6d" :question/math-academy-id "e-5573" :question/problem "Consider the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ and the subspace $S=\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2 \\}$, where\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the subspace $S$ given that the set $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2\\}$ is orthogonal." :question/worked-solution "Since the set $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{proj}_{S}\\,\\mathbf{x} = \\textrm{proj}_{\\mathbf{a}_1}\\mathbf{x} + \\textrm{proj}_{\\mathbf{a}_2}\\mathbf{x}\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1\\}$ and $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_2\\}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nproj_{a_{1}}x &= \\frac{x \\cdot a_{1}}{a_{1} \\cdot a_{1}}a_{1} = (2 \\cdot 1 + 3 \\cdot 1 + (-2) \\cdot 1)/(1^{2} + 1^{2} + 1^{2})a_{1} = 1\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\nproj_{a_{2}}x &= \\frac{x \\cdot a_{2}}{a_{2} \\cdot a_{2}}a_{2} = (2 \\cdot (-2) + 3 \\cdot 1 + (-2) \\cdot 1)/((-2)^{2} + 1^{2} + 1^{2})a_{2} =-\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nproj_{S}x &= proj_{a_{1}}x + proj_{a_{2}}x \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} + (-\\frac{1}{2})\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133517 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8eb07a32-45bd-5975-9f61-de6294d4895d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Distance Between a Vector and a Subspace Spanned by Orthogonal Vectors"}] :question/id #uuid "4985be13-9b44-5ec5-b342-00354339b902" :question/math-academy-id "e-5574" :question/problem "Consider the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ and the subspace $S=\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2 \\}$, where\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nFind the distance between $\\mathbf{x}$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2\\}$ is orthogonal." :question/worked-solution "Since the set $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{proj}_{S}\\,\\mathbf{x} = \\textrm{proj}_{\\mathbf{a}_1}\\mathbf{x} + \\textrm{proj}_{\\mathbf{a}_2}\\mathbf{x}\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1\\}$ and $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_2\\}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nproj_{a_{1}}x &= \\frac{x \\cdot a_{1}}{a_{1} \\cdot a_{1}}a_{1} = \\frac{3 \\cdot 1 + 2 \\cdot 2 + 1 \\cdot 1}{1^{2} + 2^{2} + 1^{2}}a_{1} = \\frac{4}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\nproj_{a_{2}}x &= \\frac{x \\cdot a_{2}}{a_{2} \\cdot a_{2}}a_{2} = (3 \\cdot 1 + 2 \\cdot (-1) + 1 \\cdot 1)/(1^{2} + (-1)^{2} + 1^{2})a_{2} = \\frac{2}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nproj_{S}x &= proj_{a_{1}}x + proj_{a_{2}}x \\\\\n&= \\frac{4}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} + \\frac{2}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{3}(2\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} = x^(^)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the distance between $\\mathbf{x}$ and the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd(x, x^(^)) &= ‖x - x^(^)‖ \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(x_{1} - x^(^)_{1})^{2} + (x_{2} - x^(^)_{2})^{2} + (x_{3} - x^(^)_{3})^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(3 - 2)^{2} + (2 - 2)^{2} + (1 - 2)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133580 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "acb4bd03-9b89-5775-a700-e1f3ea9fa62e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a Plane"}] :question/id #uuid "82a0e170-d916-5ae7-bd72-9b47c7287e0a" :question/math-academy-id "e-7173" :question/problem "Consider the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ and the plane $\\mathbf{r}=s\\mathbf{v}+t\\mathbf{w}$, where $s,t\\in\\mathbb R$, and\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$\nFind the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the plane given that $\\mathbf{v} \\perp \\mathbf{w}$." :question/worked-solution "Since our plane passes through the origin, it defines the subspace\n\n$$\nS=\\text{Span}\\{\\mathbf{v}, \\mathbf{w}\\}\n$$\n\nin $\\mathbb{R}^3$.\nAnd, since the set $\\{\\mathbf{v},\\mathbf{w}\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{proj}_{S}\\,\\mathbf{x} = \\textrm{proj}_{\\mathbf{v}}\\,\\mathbf{x} + \\textrm{proj}_{\\mathbf{w}}\\,\\mathbf{x}\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{v}\\}$ and $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{w}\\}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nproj_{v}x &= \\frac{x \\cdot v}{v \\cdot v}v = (13 \\cdot 2 + (-2) \\cdot 3 + (-1) \\cdot (-1))/(2^{2} + 3^{2} + (-1)^{2})v = \\frac{3}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\nproj_{w}x &= \\frac{x \\cdot w}{w \\cdot w}w = (13 \\cdot 5 + (-2) \\cdot (-3) + (-1) \\cdot 1)/(5^{2} + (-3)^{2} + 1^{2})w = 2\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nproj_{S}x &= proj_{v}x + proj_{w}x \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} + 2\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ \\frac{9}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2}\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}13 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133582 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c93bf305-85ff-5455-91ba-ae60f3c1989d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Acute Angle Between a Vector and a Subspace Spanned by Orthogonal Vectors"}] :question/id #uuid "7014f0ec-9ce3-598a-8ddb-b71a000d383f" :question/math-academy-id "e-7309" :question/problem "Consider the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ and the subspace $S=\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf v,\\mathbf w\\}$ where\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nFind the acute angle between $\\mathbf{x}$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set $\\{\\mathbf{v},\\mathbf{w}\\}$ is orthogonal." :question/worked-solution "Since the set $\\{\\mathbf{v},\\mathbf{w}\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{proj}_{S}\\,\\mathbf{x} = \\textrm{proj}_{\\mathbf{v}}\\mathbf{x} + \\textrm{proj}_{\\mathbf{w}}\\mathbf{x}\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{v}\\}$ and $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{w}\\}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nproj_{v}x &= \\frac{x \\cdot v}{v \\cdot v}v = (\\sqrt{10} \\cdot 0 + 3 \\cdot 1 + 1 \\cdot 1)/(0^{2} + (-1)^{2} + 1^{2})v = 2\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\nproj_{w}x &= \\frac{x \\cdot w}{w \\cdot w}w = (\\sqrt{10} \\cdot 0 + 3 \\cdot (-1) + 1 \\cdot 1)/(0^{2} + (-1)^{2} + (-1)^{2})w = (-1)\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nproj_{S}x &= proj_{v}x + proj_{w}x \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe acute angle between $\\mathbf{x}$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $\\mathbf{x}$ and $\\textrm{proj}_S \\mathbf{x}$ or any other non-zero vector that is parallel to $\\textrm{proj}_S \\mathbf{x}$. So, computing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos θ &= \\frac{x \\cdot proj_{S}x}{‖x‖‖proj_{S}x‖} \\\\\n&= (\\sqrt{10} \\cdot 0 + 3 \\cdot 3 + 1 \\cdot 1)/(\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{10})^{2} + 3^{2} + 1^{2}}\\sqrt{0^{2} + 3^{2} + 1^{2}}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{10}{\\sqrt{20}\\sqrt{10}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{10}{10\\sqrt{2}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\arccos\\left(\\dfrac{{\\sqrt2}}{2}\\right) = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134187 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1cae73a3-842e-5a1a-8774-35bba342845c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a Subspace Spanned by Orthogonal Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d29ebffe-7b4e-5d9f-bdba-5c1ecb74de9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131565} {:answer/id #uuid "18db8704-c3ae-55d9-aeec-2dc393a42bd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131566} {:answer/id #uuid "305f7a56-e420-5e0a-aaa1-375007e53a2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131567} {:answer/id #uuid "437131d3-209d-5440-8146-a7acea973eba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131568} {:an